\chapter{Process Scheduling \& Execution Model}

\section{M:N Threading via Scheduler Activations}

Telix decouples user-level thread scheduling from kernel-level
dispatching using $M:N$ Scheduler Activations \footcite{ande92}. The
kernel notifies the user-level runtime of blocking events (e.g., page
faults, I/O delays) via explicit upcalls, enabling user space to
schedule another ready thread without losing CPU allocations.

\section{Fair-Share Mechanics}

Preemptive task selection within a multikernel core domain is managed by
an Earliest Eligible Virtual Deadline First (EEVDF) scheduler
\footcite{stoi95}.

\section{Co-Scheduling Mechanics}

For parallel workloads spanning multiple cores, Telix coordinates core
scheduling using matrix gang scheduling techniques
\footcite{oust82, feit92} to avoid lock-step waiting across parallel
thread boundaries.
